// Negative fixture (guard reject): using a nullable without a null guard
// must be rejected before the program runs (MEMORY_GUARD check 6). Narrowing
// only ever proves NON-null, so inside the `x == null` branch the raw read
// stays illegal. The nullable must come from a maybe-null source: a
// literal-initialized `int? x = 5` is proven non-null at declaration and
// legitimately passes (declaration-state flow concept).
// Expected pre-run diagnostic: GuardError: NullValueException ...

int? src() << null;

void main() {
    int? x = src();
    if (x == null) {
        int y = x;
        printline(y);
    }
}
